JIT: entry points stop storing the block header; the cold paths find the block from the host PC

Every entry point of a block (a function's entry, the block a call returns to, an indirect branch target) ran
"adr; str" to record its JITCodeHeader in CPUState::InlineJITBlockHeader, only for the paths that rebuild RIP or patch
the block: exceptions and suspends (RestoreRIPFromHostPC), REP MOVS/STOS progress, inline self-modifying code checks
(IsAddressInCurrentBlock, IsCurrentBlockSingleInst, GetGuestBlockEntry) and the unaligned atomic handler. All of them
run with the host PC at hand. On Apple cores the store is the expensive part of call-dense code: the call/return path
is bound by stores (and the memory-ordering flushes around them), not by instruction count.

Now each CodeBuffer keeps the start offsets of the blocks compiled into it (registered in Arm64JITCore::CompileCode
and LoadCachedCode, before the block can be reached), CPUBackend::FindBlockHeader returns the block a host PC lies in
(checked against the block's tail size), and ContextImpl::SyncCurrentBlock sets InlineJITBlockHeader from it. The
users call it first (RestoreRIPFromHostPC and RestoreStringOpProgressFromHostPC do it themselves; the ARM64EC, WOW64
and Linux self-modifying-code paths and HandleUnalignedAccess call it with the faulting PC). Outside translated code
the field keeps its last value, which those users check against the PC as before. SQUARK_FEX_ENTRY_HEADER=1 brings
the store back.

TFDi MD-11 WebAssembly replay (squark md11-bench, wasm2c x64 build without the per-call depth count), per frame:
40.34M -> 37.78M instructions, 9.96M -> 9.37M cycles (median of 3, alternated), frame median 1.94 -> 1.82 ms, module
state hash unchanged. FBW A380X benchmark: 481k/478k -> 472k/472k cycles per tick. Faults at entry blocks, return
blocks, three calls deep and in hot loops report the exact RIP (squark tools/fault-test); without the lookup the first
one goes to a wrong RIP.
